Attribution
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Baton is a derivative work of the "handoff" skill from metaswarm:

    metaswarm — Multi-agent orchestration framework for Claude Code,
                Gemini CLI, and Codex CLI
    Author:   Dave Sifry
    Source:   https://github.com/dsifry/metaswarm
    License:  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Dave Sifry

The document structure, method steps, template, and anti-patterns originate
from metaswarm's handoff skill and are used under the terms of the MIT License.

Baton's additions over the original:
  - A pre-finalization Human Leverage Gate: the file is written before the
    human is asked anything, at most one round of one to three qualifying
    questions follows, and an unanswered choice is recorded as deferred by
    absence with its option-preserving default and revisit trigger, so
    non-interactive, scheduled, and subagent runs never stall.
  - Four named evidence classes — Observed, Derived, Volatile, Unknown, no
    synonyms — applied to every load-bearing claim, plus a requirement that
    every cited path resolve from the repository root or be given absolute.
  - Least-sufficient tier routing: every execution lane is assigned to a
    FAST, BALANCED, or FRONTIER model family (not a model ID, unless a real
    catalog is cited), with a one-sentence insufficiency case for any
    FRONTIER lane.
  - Runtime capability activation that enumerates the receiving harness's
    actual skills, tools, subagents, and connectors, ranks access paths, and
    recommends a missing option for the human to approve — Baton never
    installs or authorizes a capability itself.
  - One handoff template with three soft-ceiling depth labels (COMPACT,
    STANDARD, GOVERNED) instead of a separate structure per shape, so
    evidence stays proportional to the work's real uncertainty.
  - Two execution shapes, DIRECT and LEAN, with escalation past LEAN gated on
    naming a concrete coordination failure neither shape can manage.
  - A mechanical Step 8 check — an inline bash block, or the repository's own
    evals/check.sh when present — that a handoff must pass before its closing
    sentence is emitted, plus the evals/ tooling that replays that check
    across a corpus of real and generated handoffs.
  - Generalization to be harness-agnostic, tested on Claude Code and OpenAI
    Codex CLI, with no hard dependency on any orchestration framework, issue
    tracker, or plugin.

Grateful thanks to Dave Sifry and the metaswarm project.
